From: ziqianou <ziqianou@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: nf_queue: only pin skb->dev for bridged packets

The CIP backport of upstream commit e196115ec330 ("netfilter: nf_queue:
hold bridge skb->dev while queued") saves skb->dev in every queue entry
and takes a device reference on it:

  nf_queue_entry_get_refs() -> dev_hold(entry->skb_dev)

That is only safe when skb->dev is the device that is actually sending
the packet.  It is not, for locally generated IP packets: skb->dev is
assigned by ip_output() in the POST_ROUTING path, i.e. after the
LOCAL_OUT hook has already run.  A retransmitted TCP segment therefore
still carries skb->dev from its previous transmission.  If the route
changed in between (Wi-Fi <-> cellular, VPN teardown, interface
re-creation), that pointer refers to a net_device that has been
unregistered and freed, and the per-cpu refcount in dev_hold() faults:

  Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address 0000003952ba1060
  pc : nf_queue_entry_get_refs+0x4c
  Call trace:
   nf_queue_entry_get_refs
   nf_queue
   nf_hook_slow
   __ip_local_out
   __ip_queue_xmit
   __tcp_transmit_skb
   __tcp_retransmit_skb
   tcp_ack
   ...
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Fatal exception

The saved device is only required for bridged packets, where
br_pass_frame_up() rewrites skb->dev to the bridge master before the
packet is queued.  Restrict the reference to NFPROTO_BRIDGE so the IP
paths keep the original 4.19 behaviour (state->in/out are still pinned
by nf_queue_entry_get_refs()).

Signed-off-by: ziqianou <ziqianou@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 net/netfilter/nf_queue.c | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/net/netfilter/nf_queue.c b/net/netfilter/nf_queue.c
--- a/net/netfilter/nf_queue.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_queue.c
@@ -195,7 +195,14 @@ static int __nf_queue(struct sk_buff *skb, const struct nf_hook_state *state,
 
 	*entry = (struct nf_queue_entry) {
 		.skb	= skb,
-		.skb_dev = skb->dev,
+		/* Only bridged packets need skb->dev pinned here:
+		 * br_pass_frame_up() rewrites it to the bridge master.
+		 * On the IP LOCAL_OUT path skb->dev still points at the
+		 * device of the previous transmission (ip_output() sets it
+		 * later, in POST_ROUTING), so a route/interface change can
+		 * leave a freed net_device behind and dev_hold() panics.
+		 */
+		.skb_dev = (state->pf == NFPROTO_BRIDGE) ? skb->dev : NULL,
 		.state	= *state,
 		.hook_index = index,
 		.size	= sizeof(*entry) + route_key_size,
